= Solution
{parent=i}

For an <odd prime> $p$, the <Legendre symbol> is zero if $p\mid a$, one if $a$ is a nonzero <quadratic residue> modulo $p$, and minus one if it is a <quadratic nonresidue>. Thus
$$
\boxed{(a/p)\in\{0,1,-1\}.}
$$
It depends only on the congruence class of $a$ modulo $p$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <first supplementary law for quadratic reciprocity> is
$$
\boxed{(-1/p)=(-1)^{(p-1)/2}=\begin{cases}1,&p\equiv1\pmod4,\\-1,&p\equiv3\pmod4.\end{cases}}
$$
It also follows immediately from <Euler's criterion> applied to $-1$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <second supplementary law for quadratic reciprocity> is
$$
\boxed{(2/p)=(-1)^{(p^2-1)/8}=\begin{cases}1,&p\equiv1,7\pmod8,\\-1,&p\equiv3,5\pmod8.\end{cases}}
$$
The exponent is an integer because every odd square is one modulo eight.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

<Multiplicativity of the Legendre symbol> gives
$$
\boxed{(ab/p)=(a/p)(b/p).}
$$
This includes the case where $p$ divides a factor, when both sides are zero.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For distinct <odd primes> $p,q$, the <quadratic reciprocity> law is
$$
\boxed{(p/q)(q/p)=(-1)^{\frac{p-1}{2}\frac{q-1}{2}}.}
$$
Thus the two symbols have opposite signs exactly when both primes are three modulo four; otherwise they agree. Distinctness excludes zero symbols.

= b
{parent=C2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Factor $a=\prod_i q_i$ and $b=\prod_j p_j$, listing prime factors with repetitions. Coprimality ensures $q_i\ne p_j$. By the definition of the <Jacobi symbol> and <multiplicativity of the Legendre symbol>,
$$
(a/b)(b/a)=\prod_{i,j}(q_i/p_j)(p_j/q_i)
=(-1)^{\left(\sum_i(q_i-1)/2\right)\left(\sum_j(p_j-1)/2\right)}.
$$
For odd integers $r,s$,
$$
\frac{rs-1}{2}\equiv\frac{r-1}{2}+\frac{s-1}{2}\pmod2,
$$
since the difference is $(r-1)(s-1)/2$, an even integer. Inductively the two sums in the exponent are congruent to $(a-1)/2$ and $(b-1)/2$ modulo two. Hence
$$
\boxed{(a/b)(b/a)=(-1)^{\frac{a-1}{2}\frac{b-1}{2}}.}
$$
If either number is one, the empty product convention proves the same identity. This extends the prime law to arbitrary coprime positive odd denominators.

= c
{parent=C2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $p=4272943$. Since $p\equiv7\pmod8$, the supplementary laws give $(-1/p)=-1$ and $(2/p)=1$. Therefore $(-286/p)=-(143/p)$.

Both $143$ and $p$ are three modulo four. Reciprocity for the <Jacobi symbol>, followed by the supplied remainder, gives
$$
(143/p)=-(p/143)=-(103/143).
$$
Again both denominators are three modulo four, so
$$
(103/143)=-(143/103)=-(40/103).
$$
Now $103\equiv7\pmod8$ and $40=8\cdot5$. Consequently
$$
(40/103)=(2/103)^3(5/103)=(103/5)=(3/5)=-1,
$$
because the nonzero squares modulo five are one and four. Thus $(103/143)=1$, $(143/p)=-1$, and finally
$$
\boxed{(-286/p)=1.}
$$
The congruence **is soluble**, with exactly two distinct solutions modulo this prime: a nonzero root and its negative. The computation uses a prime denominator in its final step; a positive Jacobi symbol with composite denominator would not by itself imply solubility.

= D3
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D3}

The <monotone convergence theorem> states that if $0\leq h_n\uparrow h$ pointwise for measurable <functions>, then $h$ is measurable and $\int h=\lim_n\int h_n$, allowing infinity.

Apply it to the partial sums $S_N=\sum_{n=0}^Nf_n$. They are nonnegative, measurable, and increase pointwise to $f$. Finite additivity of the <integral> and monotone convergence yield
$$
\boxed{\int_{\mathbb R}f=\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{n=0}^N\int_{\mathbb R}f_n
=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\int_{\mathbb R}f_n.}
$$
The assumed finite sum of <integrals> makes $f$ integrable. This proves the series-integration rule needed below directly from the stated theorem, without assuming an additional interchange theorem.

= a
{parent=D3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $\alpha>0$, <integration by parts> on $[0,R]$ gives
$$
\int_0^R xe^{-\alpha x}\,dx
=\frac1{\alpha^2}-e^{-\alpha R}\left(\frac R\alpha+\frac1{\alpha^2}\right).
$$
The exponential remainder tends to zero. Since the integrand is nonnegative, the <monotone convergence theorem> applied to truncations identifies the improper <integral> with the Lebesgue <integral>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\infty xe^{-\alpha x}\,dx=\alpha^{-2}.}
$$
Its finite value proves integrability as well as evaluating it.

= b
{parent=D3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $x>0$, the <geometric series> gives
$$
\frac{xe^{-\alpha x}}{1-e^{-x}}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}xe^{-(n+\alpha)x}.
$$
Every summand is nonnegative and has <integral> $(n+\alpha)^{-2}$ by part (a). This numerical series converges by comparison with $\sum_{n\geq1}n^{-2}$. The series-integration rule proved above therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\infty\frac{xe^{-\alpha x}}{1-e^{-x}}\,dx
=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac1{(n+\alpha)^2}<\infty.}
$$
The value at the single endpoint zero does not affect this Lebesgue <integral>.

= c
{parent=D3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

On $0<x<1$, the geometric expansion gives summands $h_n(x)=x^{n+\beta-1}-x^{n-\beta}$. Their <integrals> are
$$
\int_0^1h_n(x)\,dx
=\frac1{n+\beta}-\frac1{n+1-\beta}
=\frac{1-2\beta}{n^2+n+\beta-\beta^2}.
$$
Both powers are integrable because $0<\beta<1$. If $\beta<1/2$, each $h_n$ is nonnegative, since its first exponent is smaller. Their <integrals> form a convergent nonnegative series: the $n=0$ term is finite and subsequent terms are bounded by a constant times $n^{-2}$. The proved series-integration rule applies.

If $\beta>1/2$, apply precisely that rule to $-h_n$, which are nonnegative, and negate the result. At $\beta=1/2$ all summands and the integrand are zero. Thus in every case the <integral> is absolutely integrable and
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1\frac{x^{\beta-1}-x^{-\beta}}{1-x}\,dx
=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1-2\beta}{n^2+n+\beta-\beta^2}.}
$$
The common sign of the summands is the reason no unproved theorem for integrating arbitrary signed series is needed.

= D4
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=D4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <null set> $E\subseteq\mathbb R$ has Lebesgue outer measure zero. Equivalently, for every $\epsilon>0$ it can be covered by countably many open intervals whose total length is less than $\epsilon$. This definition also applies before assuming $E$ measurable; every such set is Lebesgue measurable with measure zero.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A property holds **<almost everywhere>** when its exceptional set, where the property fails, is a <null set>. The exceptional set need not be empty or countable.

= b
{parent=D4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Given $\epsilon>0$, cover $E_1$ by open intervals of total length less than $\epsilon/2$, and cover $E_2$ similarly. Combining the two countable covers gives a cover of their union of total length less than $\epsilon$. Thus **$E_1\cup E_2$ is a <null set>**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each $n\geq1$, choose a countable interval cover of $E_n$ of total length below $\epsilon/2^n$. The union of these covers is countable and covers $\bigcup_n E_n$, with total length less than $\sum_n\epsilon/2^n=\epsilon$. As $\epsilon$ is arbitrary, **a countable union of <null sets> is null**.

= c
{parent=D4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The relation is reflexive because the exceptional set for $f=f$ is empty, and symmetric because the exceptional sets for $f=g$ and $g=f$ agree. If $f=g$ off a <null set> $N_1$ and $g=h$ off a <null set> $N_2$, then $f=h$ off $N_1\cup N_2$, which is null by part (b). Hence it is transitive as well, so **equality <almost everywhere> is an <equivalence relation>**.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each $n$, let $N_n$ be the <null set> where $f_n\ne g_n$. Their union $N$ is null. At any $x\notin N$, the two <sequences> agree term by term, so their <pointwise limits> are equal: $f(x)=g(x)$. Therefore **$f\sim g$**. Taking the countable union first is essential: it produces one exceptional set outside which all equalities hold simultaneously.

= d
{parent=D4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Exactly those integrable <functions> that vanish <almost everywhere>:
$$
\boxed{\|f\|_1=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad f=0\text{ almost everywhere}.}
$$
Thus this expression is a seminorm on actual <functions>, becoming a norm after the quotient in part (ii).

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $N$ be the <vector> subspace of integrable <functions> that vanish <almost everywhere>. The <Lebesgue space> is the <quotient vector space> $L^1(\mathbb R)=\mathcal L^1(\mathbb R)/N$: its elements are equivalence classes of <functions> equal <almost everywhere>. Addition and scalar multiplication are $[f]+[g]=[f+g]$ and $c[f]=[cf]$.

Set $\|[f]\|_1=\int|f|$. This is well-defined because replacing $f$ by an equal-almost-everywhere representative does not change the <integral>. It is nonnegative, homogeneous, and obeys the triangle inequality by $|f+g|\leq|f|+|g|$. Part (i) says its only zero element is the zero class. Hence **it is a <norm> on $L^1$**.

= e
{parent=D4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For each integer $m\geq1$, list the $m^2$ <functions>
$$
f_{m,j}(x)=m\,\mathbf1_{[j/m^2,(j+1)/m^2)}(x),\qquad0\leq j<m^2,
$$
in increasing $j$, and concatenate the blocks in increasing $m$. Each <function> has $L^1$ norm $1/m$, so the resulting <sequence> has norm tending to zero. Every $x\in(0,1)$ lies in exactly one interval of each block, where the <function> takes value $m$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\|f_n\|_1\to0,\qquad\limsup_n f_n(x)=\infty\quad(0<x<1).}
$$
This <unbounded typewriter sequence with vanishing L1 norm> is a moving-spike construction: the support shrinks fast enough to beat the increasing height.

= ii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No such <sequence> exists.** By <Fatou's lemma> on $(0,1)$,
$$
\int_0^1\liminf_n|g_n(x)|\,dx
\leq\liminf_n\int_0^1|g_n(x)|\,dx
\leq\liminf_n\|g_n\|_1=0.
$$
But the proposed nonnegative measurable integrand is positive at every point of $(0,1)$, so its <integral> cannot be zero. Explicitly, the sets where it is at least $1/k$ cover $(0,1)$; at least one has positive measure, forcing a positive <integral>. This contradicts the displayed bound.

= E3
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=E3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If $A$ is closed and $a_n\in A$ converges to $x\in X$, then $x$ cannot lie in the open complement: an open ball about such an $x$ would eventually contain all $a_n$ while missing $A$. Thus $x\in A$.

Conversely, suppose $A$ contains every limit of a <sequence> in $A$. If $x\in\overline A$, every ball $B(x,1/n)$ meets $A$; choose $a_n$ in that intersection. Then $a_n\to x$, so $x\in A$. Hence $\overline A=A$, proving that **a subset of a <metric space> is a <closed set> exactly when it is sequentially closed**.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A <metric space> is **<complete>** when every <Cauchy sequence> in it converges to a point of that space. A <sequence> is Cauchy when, for each $\epsilon>0$, there is $N$ such that $d(x_m,x_n)<\epsilon$ whenever $m,n\geq N$. The requirement that the limit lie in the space distinguishes completeness from having limits only in a larger ambient space.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $A$ is closed in the complete ambient <metric space>, any <Cauchy sequence> in $A$ converges in $X$ by completeness, and its limit belongs to $A$ by part (i). Thus $A$ is complete with its restricted metric.

Conversely, suppose $A$ is complete and a <sequence> $a_n\in A$ converges in $X$ to $x$. A convergent <sequence> is Cauchy, also in the restricted metric, so completeness of $A$ gives $a_n\to a$ for some $a\in A$. Uniqueness of a metric limit gives $x=a\in A$. Part (i) now makes $A$ closed. Hence
$$
\boxed{A\text{ complete}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad A\text{ closed in }X.}
$$

= b
{parent=E3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $a^{(m)}=(1,1/2,\ldots,1/m,0,0,\ldots)$. Each has finite support, and in the <supremum norm> metric it converges to $a=(1,1/2,1/3,\ldots)$, because $d(a^{(m)},a)=1/(m+1)$.

The limit is bounded but does not have finite support. Thus the finite-support subspace is not closed in $X$, and by part (a)(iii) **it is not complete**. Equivalently, the displayed <sequence> is Cauchy in that subspace but has no limit there.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Show that the subspace $C$ of convergent real <sequences> is closed in $X$. Suppose $a^{(m)}\in C$ converges uniformly in the coordinate index to $a\in X$. Given $\epsilon>0$, choose $m$ so $\sup_j|a_j-a_j^{(m)}|<\epsilon/3$. Since the fixed <sequence> $a^{(m)}$ converges, it is Cauchy, so for sufficiently large $j,k$ its coordinate difference is below $\epsilon/3$. Then
$$
|a_j-a_k|\leq|a_j-a_j^{(m)}|+|a_j^{(m)}-a_k^{(m)}|+|a_k^{(m)}-a_k|<\epsilon.
$$
Thus $a$ is a <Cauchy sequence> of real numbers and converges by completeness of $\mathbb R$. Hence $a\in C$, so $C$ is closed. Part (a)(iii) proves **the convergent-sequence subspace is complete**.

= E4
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=E4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <topological space> is **<compact>** if every <open cover> of it has a finite subcover. That is, if $X=\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i$ with all $U_i$ open, finitely many of those same <open sets> already cover $X$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If the intersection were empty, the open complements $X\setminus F_i$ would cover the <compact> space. A finite subcover would give an empty finite intersection of the corresponding $F_i$. But the sets are nested, so any finite intersection is the set with largest index, which is nonempty. This contradiction proves
$$
\boxed{\bigcap_iF_i\ne\varnothing.}
$$
This is the nested-closed-set form of the <finite intersection property> for <compact> spaces.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Suppose no $F_N$ is contained in $U$. Then $K_i=F_i\cap(X\setminus U)$ are nested nonempty closed subsets of the <compact> space. Part (ii) gives a point in their intersection. Such a point lies both in $\bigcap_iF_i$ and outside $U$, contradicting the hypothesis. Thus some $F_N\subseteq U$, and nesting gives
$$
\boxed{F_i\subseteq U\quad\text{for every }i\geq N.}
$$
<Compactness> upgrades containment of the limiting intersection to eventual containment of the whole nested sets.

= b
{parent=E4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For each $b\in B$, use the supplied <Hausdorff condition> fact to choose disjoint <open sets> $U_b,V_b$ with $A\subseteq U_b$ and $b\in V_b$. <Compactness> of $B$ gives finitely many $b_1,\ldots,b_r$ whose $V_{b_j}$ cover $B$. Set
$$
\boxed{U=\bigcap_{j=1}^rU_{b_j},\qquad V=\bigcup_{j=1}^rV_{b_j}.}
$$
Both are open; $U$ contains $A$ and $V$ contains $B$. They are disjoint because membership in $U\cap V$ would put a point in some $U_{b_j}\cap V_{b_j}$. If one <compact> set is empty, use the empty set and $X$ directly. Thus **disjoint <compact> subspaces of a <Hausdorff space> admit disjoint open neighborhoods**.

= c
{parent=E4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <topological space> is **<connected>** if it cannot be written as the union of two disjoint nonempty open subsets. Equivalently, its only subsets that are both open and closed are the empty set and the whole space. Under this definition the empty space is <connected>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $A$ is disconnected, a separation writes it as $A=A_1\cup A_2$ with $A_1,A_2$ disjoint, nonempty and open in its <subspace topology>. By definition there are ambient <open sets> $U,V$ with $A_1=U\cap A$ and $A_2=V\cap A$. These give exactly the four conditions stated.

Conversely, those conditions make $U\cap A,V\cap A$ two nonempty disjoint relatively <open sets> whose union is $A$. They are therefore a separation. This proves the equivalence. **The ambient sets may overlap outside $A$**, which is why the condition here is $U\cap V\cap A=\varnothing$.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $A$ is disconnected, take a separation $A=A_1\cup A_2$. Each piece is also relatively closed, being the complement of the other. They are <compact> as closed subsets of the <compact> space $A$.

Part (b), using the ambient Hausdorff property, gives disjoint ambient open neighborhoods of $A_1$ and $A_2$. They meet $A$, cover it, and are disjoint everywhere, as required. Conversely, such ambient <open sets> give a separation on intersecting with $A$, by part (ii). Hence **<compactness> and the Hausdorff property permit fully disjoint ambient neighborhoods**.

= d
{parent=E4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Put $K=\bigcap_iF_i$. If some $F_i$ is empty, so is $K$, which is <connected>. Otherwise the <compact> sets are closed in the Hausdorff ambient space, and can all be considered as nested nonempty closed subsets of the <compact> space $F_0$. Part (a)(ii) gives $K\ne\varnothing$, and $K$ is <compact>.

Suppose $K$ were disconnected. Part (c)(iii) gives disjoint ambient <open sets> $U,V$ covering $K$, both meeting it. Part (a)(iii), applied within $F_0$ to $U\cup V$, gives $F_N\subseteq U\cup V$ for some $N$. Both $U$ and $V$ meet $F_N$ because they meet $K\subseteq F_N$. Their intersections with $F_N$ would therefore disconnect $F_N$, a contradiction. Thus
$$
\boxed{K\text{ is connected}.}
$$

= F2
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=F2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <total derivative> of $F$ at $x$ is the <linear map> $DF_x:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m$ such that
$$
\boxed{F(x+h)=F(x)+DF_x(h)+o(\|h\|).}
$$
The <Jacobian matrix> is the $m\times n$ <matrix> $J_F(x)=(\partial F_i/\partial x_j)(x)$. When the total <derivative> exists, this is its <matrix> in standard bases: $DF_x(h)=J_F(x)h$. Existence of partial <derivatives> alone need not imply existence of the total <derivative>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The map is **<continuously differentiable>** if it is differentiable at every point of $U$ and the <derivative> map $x\mapsto DF_x$ is continuous. In finite dimensions this is equivalent to all first partial <derivatives> of all its components existing and being continuous on $U$. The class is denoted $C^1(U,\mathbb R^m)$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $F:U\subseteq\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at $x$, and $G$ is defined on a neighborhood of $F(x)$ and differentiable there, the <chain rule> states
$$
\boxed{D(G\circ F)_x=DG_{F(x)}\circ DF_x.}
$$
In standard coordinates the same statement is
$$
\boxed{J_{G\circ F}(x)=J_G(F(x))J_F(x).}
$$
The evaluation point in the first factor is $F(x)$, and the <matrix> order follows the order in which the <linear maps> act on increments.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For an increment $h$, direct expansion and the <chain rule> give
$$
\boxed{DF_x(h)=Ah,\qquad Dg_x(h)=2x^Th,\qquad Dk_x(h)=2(Ax)^TAh=2x^TA^TAh.}
$$
For example, $\|x+h\|^2-\|x\|^2=2x^Th+\|h\|^2$, so the quadratic remainder divided by $\|h\|$ tends to zero. The corresponding <gradients> are $\nabla g(x)=2x$ and $\nabla k(x)=2A^TAx$; the <derivative> of $F$ is the constant <matrix> $A$.

= b
{parent=F2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Lagrange multiplier>[Lagrange multiplier theorem] for one regular constraint says: if $f,k$ are $C^1$ near $x$, $f(x)=0$, $\nabla f(x)\ne0$, and $x$ is a local extremum of $k$ restricted to $f^{-1}(0)$, then
$$
\boxed{\nabla k(x)=\lambda\nabla f(x)\quad\text{for some }\lambda\in\mathbb R.}
$$
For several constraints with linearly independent <gradients>, the objective <gradient> lies in their linear span. The regularity assumption rules out singular points where the usual multiplier conclusion can fail.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the constraint set, $\|x\|=1$, so $\nabla f(x)=2x\ne0$. Part (a)(iv) and the <Lagrange multiplier>[Lagrange multiplier theorem] give $2A^TAx=2\lambda x$, hence
$$
\boxed{A^TAx=\lambda x.}
$$
Since $x$ is nonzero, it is an <eigenvector>. Moreover $\lambda=x^TA^TAx=\|Ax\|^2\geq0$, so the associated <eigenvalue> is real and nonnegative.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $n\geq1$, the unit sphere is nonempty and <compact> by the <Heine-Borel theorem>. The <function> $k(x)=\|Ax\|^2$ is continuous and therefore attains a minimum and a maximum on it by the <extreme value theorem>. Either point supplies a nonzero <eigenvector> in part (ii), with a real multiplier. Thus **$A^TA$ always has a real <eigenvalue>**, indeed a nonnegative one. This gives existence without assuming the spectral theorem first.

= G2
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=G2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For fixed endpoint variations, the <Euler-Lagrange equations> are
$$
\boxed{\frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}-\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}=0,\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.}
$$
Indeed the <first variation> is $\int\sum_i(L_{q_i}\delta q_i+L_{\dot q_i}\delta\dot q_i)dt$. <Integration by parts> removes the <derivatives> from the variations; their zero endpoint values remove the boundary term. The fundamental lemma then makes every remaining coefficient vanish.

= b
{parent=G2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The autonomous <Lagrangian> has the <Beltrami identity>
$$
\sum_i\dot q_iL_{\dot q_i}-L=\text{constant}.
$$
Here $L$ is homogeneous of degree two in the velocities, so $\dot uL_{\dot u}+\dot vL_{\dot v}=2L$. Thus **$L$ itself is constant**, say $C\geq0$.

For $C>0$, put $\ell=\sqrt L$. Its Euler-Lagrange expression along the curve is
$$
\frac{d}{dt}\ell_{\dot q_i}-\ell_{q_i}
=\frac1{2\sqrt L}\left(\frac{d}{dt}L_{\dot q_i}-L_{q_i}\right)
-\frac{\dot L}{4L^{3/2}}L_{\dot q_i}=0.
$$
The first term vanishes by stationarity for $L$, and the second by constancy of $L$. Thus the same path is stationary for its length functional $\int\sqrt L\,dt$.

On a regular segment described as a graph $v=v(u)$, reparametrize by $u$. Since $\dot v=v_u\dot u$,
$$
\sqrt L\,dt=\sqrt{E+2Fv_u+Gv_u^2}\,|du|.
$$
Choose the orientation with increasing $u$; reversing it does not change stationarity. Invariance of length under regular reparametrization therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\delta\int\sqrt{E+2Fv_u+Gv_u^2}\,du=0.}
$$
This is the principle of <length extremals from a constant quadratic energy>. The stated nonnegativity also permits the degenerate case $C=0$. Then the length is zero, its absolute minimum since the integrand is nonnegative, so it is an extremal in the minimizing sense. The differentiated square-root Euler-Lagrange equation above is only used where $L>0$; it would not be legitimate to divide by $\sqrt L$ on a null curve. For a genuine surface metric and a regular nonconstant path, $C>0$ automatically.

= c
{parent=G2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the <surface of revolution> $r(\eta,\zeta)=(\eta,f(\eta)\cos\zeta,f(\eta)\sin\zeta)$, the coordinate tangent <vectors> have squared lengths $1+f'^2$ and $f^2$, and their dot product is zero. Hence its <first fundamental form> is
$$
\boxed{ds^2=A(\eta)d\eta^2+f(\eta)^2d\zeta^2,\qquad A=1+f'^2.}
$$
Use the energy <Lagrangian> $L=A\dot\eta^2+f^2\dot\zeta^2$. Its Euler-Lagrange equations are
$$
2A\ddot\eta+A'\dot\eta^2-2ff'\dot\zeta^2=0,\qquad
\frac d{dt}(2f^2\dot\zeta)=0.
$$
For a meridian $\zeta=\zeta_0$, choose arc length as parameter, so $\dot\eta=1/\sqrt A$. Differentiating gives $2A\ddot\eta+A'\dot\eta^2=0$, while the second equation is automatic. Part (b) makes this path an extremal of length. Thus **every meridian is a <geodesic>**.

For a nonconstant parallel $\eta=\eta_0$, choose constant nonzero $\dot\zeta$. The second equation holds, and the first reduces to $f(\eta_0)f'(\eta_0)\dot\zeta^2=0$. Since $f>0$, this is equivalent to
$$
\boxed{f'(\eta_0)=0.}
$$
Conversely that condition makes both equations hold at constant speed, hence supplies a length extremal. Geometrically, a parallel is geodesic exactly at a stationary radius of the surface.

= H2
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=H2}

Differentiating orthonormality gives $\dot e_i\cdot e_j=-e_i\cdot\dot e_j$. With $\omega=\tfrac12\sum_i e_i\times\dot e_i$, the <vector> triple product yields
$$
\omega\times e_j=\tfrac12\left(\dot e_j-\sum_i e_i(\dot e_i\cdot e_j)\right)=\dot e_j.
$$
The body coordinates $x_i$ of a fixed material point are constant. Differentiating its position $R_G+\sum_ix_ie_i$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{v=v_G+\omega\times r.}
$$
This identifies $\omega$ as the <angular velocity> of the <rigid body>.

Because $G$ is the <center of mass>, $\int\rho r\,dV=0$, so the translational contribution to the <angular momentum> about $G$ vanishes. The remaining <vector> triple product gives
$$
L=\int\rho\{(r\cdot r)\omega-(r\cdot\omega)r\}\,dV,
\qquad
\boxed{J_{ij}=\int\rho\left(|r|^2\delta_{ij}-x_ix_j\right)dV,\quad L_i=\sum_jJ_{ij}\omega_j.}
$$
This symmetric <matrix> is the <inertia tensor> about $G$. The angular-momentum balance about the center of mass is $dL/dt=\tau_{\mathrm{ext},G}$ in an inertial frame. With no external forces, that torque is zero, so **the inertial <vector> $L$ is constant**. Its components in rotating body axes need not individually be constant.

For the tool, let the disc lie at $z=0$ and the rod extend to $z=l=\sqrt3a$. The total mass is $3M$ and its center is at $z_G=l/3$. Axial symmetry makes the <inertia tensor> diagonal with $I_x=I_y$.

The standard disc moments are $MR^2/2$ about its normal and $MR^2/4$ about a diameter. A thin rod has moment $ml^2/12$ about a transverse axis through its center. The <parallel axis theorem> adds $md^2$ for a parallel shift of distance $d$. Therefore
$$
I_z=\tfrac12M(2a)^2=2Ma^2,
$$
and
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_x=I_y
&=Ma^2+M(l/3)^2+\frac{2Ml^2}{12}+2M(l/6)^2\\
&=Ma^2+\frac{Ml^2}{3}=2Ma^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The rod contributes zero about its own longitudinal axis. Thus $\boxed{J=2Ma^2I_3}$, an isotropic <inertia tensor> even though the shape is not spherical. This gives a <nonspherical rigid body with isotropic inertia>. Consequently $L=2Ma^2\omega$. Conservation of $L$ in torque-free motion proves **the <angular velocity> is constant for every initial orientation and spin**.

= J2
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=J2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Set $r_0=q\mathcal E/(m\omega^2)$ and $y=r-r_0$. Completing the square in the <potential energy> gives
$$
\frac12m\omega^2|r|^2-q\mathcal E\cdot r
=\frac12m\omega^2|y|^2-\frac{q^2|\mathcal E|^2}{2m\omega^2}.
$$
Translation leaves the <Laplacian> unchanged, and the shifted three-dimensional <quantum harmonic oscillator> separates into three one-dimensional oscillators. Their energies add, giving
$$
\boxed{E_N=(N+\tfrac32)\hbar\omega-\frac{q^2|\mathcal E|^2}{2m\omega^2},\qquad N=n_1+n_2+n_3.}
$$
The <energy-level degeneracy> is the dimension of the energy eigenspace, or number of linearly independent states with that energy. Counting nonnegative integer triples of total $N$ by <stars and bars> gives
$$
\boxed{d_N=\binom{N+2}{2}=\frac{(N+1)(N+2)}2.}
$$
The constant electric <field> displaces the oscillator and shifts all levels by the same amount, preserving their degeneracies.

= b
{parent=J2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $\gamma=m\omega/\hbar$. Substitution of $\psi=F(y)e^{-\gamma y^2/2}$ in the one-dimensional <Schrödinger equation> with energy $(n+1/2)\hbar\omega$ gives
$$
F''-2\gamma yF'+2\gamma nF=0.
$$
For degrees zero, one and two, coefficient comparison yields, up to independent nonzero constants,
$$
\boxed{F_0=1,\qquad F_1=y,\qquad F_2=y^2-\frac1{2\gamma}.}
$$
For the shifted coordinates $y_i=x_i-(r_0)_i$, let $G(y)=e^{-\gamma|y|^2/2}$. A basis of <wave functions> for the first three energy levels is:

* $N=0$: $\boxed{G}$.
* $N=1$: $\boxed{y_1G,\ y_2G,\ y_3G}$.
* $N=2$: $\boxed{(y_i^2-1/(2\gamma))G\ (i=1,2,3),\quad y_1y_2G,\ y_1y_3G,\ y_2y_3G}$.

These are products of the one-dimensional <polynomial> solutions. Arbitrary linear combinations within each listed eigenspace are also stationary states. Their counts $1,3,6$ agree with part (a), so the degeneracies have all been included.

= c
{parent=J2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <Gaussian integral> gives $\int_{\mathbb R^3}e^{-\gamma|y|^2}\,d^3y=(\pi/\gamma)^{3/2}$. Thus the normalized <ground state> is, up to an overall phase,
$$
\boxed{\psi_0(r)=\left(\frac{m\omega}{\pi\hbar}\right)^{3/4}
\exp\left[-\frac{m\omega}{2\hbar}\left|r-\frac{q\mathcal E}{m\omega^2}\right|^2\right].}
$$
Its <probability> density is even in each shifted coordinate, so all three expectations $\langle y_i\rangle$ vanish by oddness. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\langle r\rangle=\frac{q\mathcal E}{m\omega^2}.}
$$
The mean position is the minimum of the displaced potential, rather than the original coordinate origin.

= K3
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=K3}

Orient $\partial B$ counterclockwise, and define positive <circulation> by that orientation. The outward normal from the body satisfies $n\,ds=(dy,-dx)$. Pressure acts inward, so
$$
F_x-iF_y=-\oint p(dy+i\,dx)=-i\oint p\,d\overline z.
$$
The steady <Bernoulli equation> for this constant-density irrotational flow is $p+\rho|v|^2/2=C$. The constant has zero <integral> around a closed curve. With <complex potential of a two-dimensional potential flow> $w=\phi+i\psi$, let $W=w'=u-iv$. The no-penetration condition makes the boundary a streamline, so $dw=Wdz$ is real along it. Thus $Wdz=\overline Wd\overline z$, and $|W|^2d\overline z=W^2dz$. Substitution proves **<Blasius force theorem>**:
$$
\boxed{F_x-iF_y=\frac{i\rho}{2}\oint_{\partial B}(w'(z))^2\,dz.}
$$
At infinity the <Laurent expansion> has the form
$$
W(z)=U-iV+\frac{\Gamma}{2\pi iz}+O(z^{-2}).
$$
There is no source term because the impermeable body has zero net flux. The circulation term follows from $\oint Wdz=\Gamma$. The coefficient of $z^{-1}$ in $W^2$ is $\Gamma(U-iV)/(\pi i)$. Analyticity in the fluid lets the body contour be enlarged to a large counterclockwise circle, so the <residue theorem> gives $\oint W^2dz=2\Gamma(U-iV)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{(F_x,F_y)=\rho\Gamma(V,-U).}
$$
Its component along $(U,V)$ is zero: **the drag is zero**. The lift magnitude is $\boxed{\rho|\Gamma|\sqrt{U^2+V^2}}$. With the stated positive counterclockwise circulation, the signed lift along the left normal $(-V,U)/\sqrt{U^2+V^2}$ is $-\rho\Gamma\sqrt{U^2+V^2}$. Reversing the circulation convention reverses this signed formula. This is the <Kutta-Joukowski theorem> with its orientation made explicit.

= K4
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=K4}

The factor $\eta_{xx}/(1+\eta_x^2)^{3/2}$ is the signed <curvature> of the graph, so the pressure jump is surface tension times curvature, the planar <Young-Laplace equation>.

Take total velocity potentials $\Phi_-=\phi_-$ and $\Phi_+=Ux+\phi_+$. Each disturbance potential satisfies the <Laplace equation>. The interface moves with the fluid: for $F=y-\eta(x,t)$, the material equation $F_t+v\cdot\nabla F=0$ gives $\eta_t+u\eta_x=v_y$. Keeping only first-order terms and evaluating at $y=0$ yields
$$
\boxed{\phi_{-,y}=\eta_t,\qquad\phi_{+,y}=\eta_t+U\eta_x.}
$$
The unsteady <Bernoulli equation> is $\Phi_t+|\nabla\Phi|^2/2+p/\rho=C(t)$, with any common gravitational contribution canceling across this equal-density interface. Subtracting the basic flow and linearizing gives $p'_-= -\rho\phi_{-,t}$ and $p'_+=-\rho(\phi_{+,t}+U\phi_{+,x})$. The linearized pressure jump is $T\eta_{xx}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\phi_{-,t}-(\phi_{+,t}+U\phi_{+,x})=(T/\rho)\eta_{xx}.}
$$
Use complex modes with real parts understood:
$$
\eta=Ae^{i(kx-\omega t)},\qquad
\phi_-=B_-e^{ky}e^{i(kx-\omega t)},\quad
\phi_+=B_+e^{-ky}e^{i(kx-\omega t)}.
$$
The decaying vertical factors solve the Laplace equation in their respective half-planes. The kinematic conditions give $B_-=-i\omega A/k$ and $B_+=i(\omega-kU)A/k$. Substitution in the dynamic condition gives
$$
\boxed{\omega^2+(\omega-kU)^2=\frac T\rho k^3,\qquad
\omega=\frac{kU}{2}\pm\sqrt{\frac{Tk^3}{2\rho}-\frac{k^2U^2}{4}}.}
$$
When the discriminant is nonnegative, the <phase speeds> are $U/2\pm\sqrt{Tk/(2\rho)-U^2/4}$. They are two dispersive capillary-wave branches advected by the mean velocity $U/2$.

When $\boxed{U^2>2Tk/\rho}$, the frequencies are complex conjugates and one mode grows exponentially, with growth rate $k\sqrt{U^2/4-Tk/(2\rho)}$. This is <Kelvin-Helmholtz instability>. Surface tension stabilizes sufficiently short waves, while for any nonzero shear, sufficiently long waves are unstable in this gravity-free equal-density model. A real cosine with real frequency describes the stable branches; the unstable case is represented by the real part of the corresponding growing complex mode.

= M4
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <Markov chain> is **<irreducible Markov chain>[irreducible]** if every state communicates with every other: for any $i,j$ there is an integer $n\geq0$ with $p_{ij}^{(n)}>0$. Thus any state can be reached from any other in finitely many steps with positive <probability>.

= b
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Every state reaches state one by deterministic downward steps. From one, the chain can reach a given state $j$ exactly when it can jump to some $k\geq j$ with $\alpha_k>0$ and then count down to $j$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{Irreducible}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\{k:\alpha_k>0\}\text{ is unbounded}.}
$$
If the support is bounded by $M$, states above $M$ cannot be reached from one, so the chain on the stated infinite state space is not irreducible. Its smaller closed class can still have a <stationary distribution>, as in part (e).

= c
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

After a jump from one to $k$, the first return to one occurs exactly $k$ steps later, with <probability> $\alpha_k$. Later return times are sums of such positive-support values. Thus their greatest common divisor is
$$
d=\gcd\{k:\alpha_k>0\}.
$$
In an irreducible chain this is the common <period of a state in a Markov chain>[period] of every state. Hence **the chain is <aperiodic> exactly when $d=1$**. A self-loop at one is sufficient but not necessary; support at two and three also has greatest common divisor one.

= d
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The first return time $\tau_1$ has distribution $\mathbb P_1(\tau_1=k)=\alpha_k$. It is finite almost surely, so state one is recurrent. It is <positive recurrent> precisely when its mean return time is finite:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_1\tau_1=\sum_{k\geq1}k\alpha_k<\infty.}
$$
By irreducibility the same recurrence class contains every state. Thus the condition is finiteness of the <expected value> of the positive-integer distribution $(\alpha_k)$. If that mean is infinite, the chain is null recurrent, not transient.

= e
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The stationary balance equations on the closed class $\{1,\ldots,M\}$ are $\pi_j=\pi_{j+1}+\pi_1/M$, with $\pi_{M+1}=0$. Working backward gives $\pi_j=\pi_1(M-j+1)/M$.

Normalization yields $1=\pi_1(M+1)/2$, so
$$
\boxed{\pi_j=\frac{2(M-j+1)}{M(M+1)}\quad(1\leq j\leq M),\qquad\pi_j=0\quad(j>M).}
$$
These probabilities sum to one and satisfy each balance equation. In particular $\pi_1=2/(M+1)$ is the reciprocal of the mean return time $(M+1)/2$, consistent with part (d).

= f
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Write a first return time as $2J$, where $\mathbb P(J=j)=2^{-j}$ for $j\geq1$. It has mean $2\cdot2=4$ and period two, so the stationary <probability> at one is $1/4$. The periodic convergence theorem would give limits $1/2$ along even times and zero along odd times.

Here one can obtain them directly. Put $u_n=\mathbb P_1(X_{2n}=1)$, with $u_0=1$. Splitting according to the first return gives the <renewal equation>
$$
u_n=\sum_{j=1}^n2^{-j}u_{n-j}.
$$
Induction gives $u_n=1/2$ for every $n\geq1$: the right side is $2^{-n}+\tfrac12\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}2^{-j}=1/2$. Odd-time returns are impossible because every return interval is even. Therefore the exact set of limit points is
$$
\boxed{\{0,\tfrac12\}.}
$$
The <sequence> does not converge to the stationary mass $1/4$, precisely because it is periodic.

= M5
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=M5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <moment generating function> is $M_X(t)=\mathbb E[e^{tX}]$ on the set where this expectation is finite. For independent, identically distributed summands, independence factors the expectation:
$$
\boxed{M_{S_n}(t)=\mathbb E\prod_{i=1}^ne^{tX_i}=\prod_{i=1}^nM(t)=M(t)^n.}
$$
The identity holds wherever the factors are finite; in the remaining parts the bounded summands make them finite for every real $t$.

= b
{parent=M5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Chebyshev's inequality> states $\mathbb P(|Y-\mu|\geq r)\leq\sigma^2/r^2$ for $r>0$ when $Y$ has mean $\mu$ and finite <variance> $\sigma^2$. Here each summand has mean zero and <variance> one, so independence gives $\mathbb ES_n=0$ and $\operatorname{Var}S_n=n$. Taking $r=an$ gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(|S_n|\geq an)\leq\min\left(1,\frac1{a^2n}\right).}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The two-point distribution gives $M(t)=(e^t+e^{-t})/2=\cosh t$. In the power series, $(2j)!=\prod_{r=1}^j(2r)(2r-1)\geq2^jj!$, so each coefficient satisfies
$$
\frac{t^{2j}}{(2j)!}\leq\frac{(t^2/2)^j}{j!}.
$$
Summing the nonnegative terms proves $\boxed{M(t)\leq e^{t^2/2}}$ for every real $t$. Part (a) therefore gives
$$
\boxed{M_{S_n}(t)\leq e^{nt^2/2}.}
$$
This is the <sub-Gaussian random variable>[sub-Gaussian] moment bound for a symmetric random sign.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $t>0$, apply <Markov's inequality> to the nonnegative random variable $e^{tS_n}$. The exponential is then increasing, so
$$
\mathbb P(S_n\geq an)\leq e^{-tan}\mathbb E[e^{tS_n}]
\leq\exp\left[n\left(\frac{t^2}{2}-at\right)\right].
$$
At $t=0$ the right side is one. For $t<0$ it exceeds one because $a>0$, so the same displayed bound remains true trivially; the upper-tail Markov argument itself uses $t>0$.

Complete the square $t^2/2-at=(t-a)^2/2-a^2/2$. Choosing $t=a$ gives the strongest bound from this family, $\mathbb P(S_n\geq an)\leq e^{-na^2/2}$. The distribution is symmetric, so the same bound holds for $S_n\leq-an$. A union bound gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(|S_n|\geq an)\leq2e^{-na^2/2}.}
$$
One may also cap this at one. Its exponential decay is stronger than the <polynomial> Chebyshev bound for fixed $a>0$ and large $n$.

= O4
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The standardized observations $X_i/\sigma$ are independent standard <normal random variables>. By the definition of the <chi-squared distribution>,
$$
Q=\frac{\sum_iX_i^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi_n^2.
$$
Its distribution is continuous, so $F_n(c_2)-F_n(c_1)=1-\alpha$ gives the required <probability>. Put $S=\sum_iX_i^2$. Solving $c_1<S/\sigma^2<c_2$ for the positive <variance> reverses the reciprocal order. The exact <confidence interval> is
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac S{F_n^{-1}(1-\alpha/2)},\frac S{F_n^{-1}(\alpha/2)}\right).}
$$
The mean is known to be zero, so the degrees of freedom are $n$, not $n-1$. Coverage is exactly $1-\alpha$ under the stated normal model.

= b
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Neyman-Pearson lemma> compares two simple hypotheses with densities $f_0,f_1$. Among all tests of size at most $\alpha$, a most powerful test rejects when $f_1(x)/f_0(x)>c$, accepts when it is below $c$, and may randomize on equality to attain the prescribed size. The threshold and any boundary randomization are chosen so the rejection <probability> under $H_0$ is $\alpha$.

The key inequality is $(\phi^*-\phi)(f_1-cf_0)\geq0$ pointwise for the likelihood-ratio test $\phi^*$. Integration shows its power is at least that of any competing test with no larger size. With continuous likelihood-ratio statistics, boundary randomization is unnecessary.

= c
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $S=\sum_i x_i^2$, the <likelihood ratio> in favor of the larger <variance> is
$$
\frac{f_{\sigma_1^2}(x)}{f_{\sigma_0^2}(x)}
=\left(\frac{\sigma_0}{\sigma_1}\right)^n
\exp\left[\frac S2\left(\frac1{\sigma_0^2}-\frac1{\sigma_1^2}\right)\right].
$$
Because $\sigma_1>\sigma_0$, this is strictly increasing in $S$. The <Neyman-Pearson lemma> therefore selects an upper-tail rejection region. Under the null, $S/\sigma_0^2\sim\chi_n^2$, so size $\alpha$ is obtained with
$$
\boxed{C=\{S\geq k_\alpha\},\qquad k_\alpha=\sigma_0^2F_n^{-1}(1-\alpha).}
$$
Continuity of the chi-squared distribution makes the boundary have <probability> zero. This is a <fixed-mean normal variance test>.

= d
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**Yes, it is a <uniformly most powerful test> for the one-sided alternative.** For every fixed $\sigma_1^2>\sigma_0^2$, the likelihood ratio is increasing in the same statistic $S$. The size-$\alpha$ threshold found in part (c) depends only on the null <variance> and $\alpha$, not on the selected alternative <variance>. Thus one and the same test is most powerful against each simple member of the composite alternative, which is exactly uniform most powerfulness.

= e
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

At a true <variance> $\sigma^2>0$, $S/\sigma^2\sim\chi_n^2$. Therefore the <power function> is
$$
\boxed{w(\sigma^2)=1-F_n\left(\frac{\sigma_0^2}{\sigma^2}F_n^{-1}(1-\alpha)\right).}
$$
The argument of $F_n$ decreases as $\sigma^2$ increases. Since the chi-squared density is positive on the positive half-line, the power is continuous and strictly increasing, with
$$
\boxed{w(0^+)=0,\qquad w(\sigma_0^2)=\alpha,\qquad w(\infty)=1.}
$$
These features determine the requested rough sketch. The curve passes through the size at the null value and approaches one for very large <variance>.

\Image[2013-variance-test-power.png]
{title=Power rises from zero through the test size to one as the <variance> increases}
{description=Original illustrative power curve for n=4 and alpha=0.05, plotted against the ratio of the true <variance> to the null <variance>. The marked point has ratio one and power alpha.}

= O5
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Ignoring a factor independent of the probabilities, the <likelihood function> is $L(\pi)=\prod_i\pi_i^{n_i}$, so its log is $\ell(\pi)=\sum_i n_i\log\pi_i$ subject to $\sum_i\pi_i=1$. A <Lagrange multiplier> gives $n_i/\pi_i=\lambda$. Summing $\pi_i=n_i/\lambda$ makes $\lambda=n$.

Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat\pi_i=n_i/n.}
$$
Since all counts are positive, a zero fitted cell <probability> would have zero likelihood. On the interior the log-likelihood is strictly concave, so this stationary point is its unique global maximum.

= b
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The unrestricted likelihood is maximized at the empirical proportions, while the null likelihood is maximized at $\pi_i(\widehat\theta)$. Twice the log of their ratio is
$$
\boxed{\Lambda(\widehat\theta)=2\sum_i n_i\log\frac{n_i}{n\pi_i(\widehat\theta)}.}
$$
Large values mean the fitted null likelihood is much smaller than the unrestricted maximum, so rejection is in the upper tail.

For a regular identifiable scalar parameter lying in the interior, positive true cell probabilities, and sufficiently large expected counts, <Wilks' theorem> gives an approximate <chi-squared distribution> with $(k-1)-1=k-2$ degrees of freedom under the null. The unrestricted <probability> <vector> has dimension $k-1$ and the fitted null has dimension one. At significance level $\alpha$, reject if $\Lambda(\widehat\theta)>F_{k-2}^{-1}(1-\alpha)$, or report the approximate p-value $1-F_{k-2}(\Lambda)$.

Cells with very small expected counts may require a prespecified pooling scheme or simulation calibration; the asymptotic reference is not an exact finite-sample law. For a regular null with $d$ fitted parameters, the corresponding degrees of freedom are $k-1-d$.

= c
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a fully specified parameter value, do not maximize within the null. Substitute $\theta_0$ directly:
$$
\boxed{\Lambda(\theta_0)=2\sum_i n_i\log\frac{n_i}{n\pi_i(\theta_0)}.}
$$
Under the same regularity and large-count conditions, compare with $\chi_{k-1}^2$, since no null parameter has been estimated. Reject in its upper tail at the desired significance level. Fixing the parameter increases the reference degrees of freedom by one relative to part (b).

= d
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The final cell is a grouped tail. Thus use twelve probabilities
$$
p_i(\theta)=e^{-\theta}\frac{\theta^i}{i!}\quad(0\leq i\leq10),\qquad
p_{11}(\theta)=\mathbb P_\theta(X\geq11)=1-\sum_{i=0}^{10}p_i(\theta).
$$
Maximize the grouped <grouped Poisson likelihood>
$$
\ell(\theta)=\sum_{i=0}^{10}n_i\log p_i(\theta)+n_{11}\log p_{11}(\theta),
$$
then take $\boxed{\widehat\pi_i=p_i(\widehat\theta)}$ for all twelve cells. For example, the interior likelihood equation is
$$
\sum_{i=0}^{10}n_i\left(\frac i\theta-1\right)
+n_{11}\frac{p_{10}(\theta)}{p_{11}(\theta)}=0,
$$
because $p_{11}'(\theta)=p_{10}(\theta)$. It can be solved numerically, with no numerical work required here.

It would be incorrect to treat the last six observations as exactly eleven. If the underlying ungrouped particle counts were available, their sample mean would be the usual Poisson maximum likelihood estimate. For the data as supplied, the grouped likelihood handles the unknown tail counts directly.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

There are twelve cells and one fitted parameter, so the likelihood-ratio reference is approximately $\chi_{10}^2$. The supplied value gives
$$
\boxed{p\text{-value}\approx1-F_{10}(14)=0.18.}
$$
Thus **do not reject the Poisson goodness-of-fit model at the conventional 5% level**; it would also not be rejected at 10%. The data do not provide strong evidence of a departure from this model. This is a compatibility conclusion, not proof that the Poisson model is true. A fully specified Poisson mean would instead use eleven degrees of freedom, which is not the fitted situation here.

= P3
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With the <weighted inner product> $\langle p,q\rangle_w=\int_{-1}^1p(x)q(x)w(x)\,dx$, a family of <orthogonal polynomials> has $\deg p_j=j$, each $p_j$ nonzero, and
$$
\boxed{\langle p_j,p_k\rangle_w=0\quad(j\ne k).}
$$
Continuity and positivity of the weight make the <integral> finite and give $\langle p_j,p_j\rangle_w>0$. Unit norm or monic leading coefficient is an additional optional normalization.

= b
{parent=P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The weight is even, so opposite-parity <polynomials> are automatically orthogonal. Set $p_0=1$, $p_1=x$, then subtract lower-degree components within each parity class. The needed moments are
$$
\mu_0=\frac83,\qquad\mu_2=\frac{16}{15},\qquad\mu_4=\frac{24}{35}.
$$
Thus $p_2=x^2-\mu_2/\mu_0=x^2-2/5$ is orthogonal to $p_0$, and $p_3=x^3-(\mu_4/\mu_2)x=x^3-9x/14$ is orthogonal to $p_1$. Parity supplies every other pair. One valid answer is
$$
\boxed{p_0=1,\quad p_1=x,\quad p_2=x^2-\frac25,\quad p_3=x^3-\frac9{14}x.}
$$
All have the required degree and positive norm; arbitrary nonzero rescalings are equally valid.

= c
{parent=P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The degree-zero <polynomial> is constant and therefore even. Assume inductively that $p_j(-x)=(-1)^jp_j(x)$ for $j<k$. Define $q_k(x)=(-1)^kp_k(-x)$, which has the same leading coefficient as $p_k$.

Changing variable $x\mapsto-x$ and using the even weight and inductive hypothesis gives $\langle q_k,p_j\rangle_w=(-1)^{k+j}\langle p_k,p_j\rangle_w=0$ for $j<k$. Hence $q_k-p_k$ has degree below $k$ and is orthogonal to every $p_0,\ldots,p_{k-1}$. Those <polynomials> form a basis for the <polynomials> of degree below $k$, so the difference is orthogonal to itself. Positivity of the weighted <inner product> forces it to be zero.

Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_k(-x)=(-1)^kp_k(x),}
$$
completing the induction and proving <parity of orthogonal polynomials for an even weight>. Thus even indices give even <functions> and odd indices give odd <functions>, regardless of the nonzero normalization chosen.

= P4
{c}
{parent=2013}
{scope}

= a
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A real square <matrix> is <orthogonal matrix>[orthogonal] when $Q^TQ=I$, equivalently when its columns form an orthonormal basis. Taking <determinants> gives $(\det Q)^2=1$, so
$$
\boxed{\det Q\in\{1,-1\}.}
$$
In particular it is invertible with inverse $Q^T$ and preserves the Euclidean norm.

= b
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <QR decomposition> is $\boxed{A=QR}$ with $Q$ orthogonal and $R$ upper triangular. One algorithm for computing it is **<Householder reflection> reduction**; a <sequence> of such reflections produces a QR factorization. The diagonal of $R$ may include zero when $A$ is singular, and no nonsingularity assumption is needed here.

= c
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Comparing the $j$th columns of $A=QR$ gives $a_j=Qr_j$. Thus
$$
\|a_j\|^2=r_j^TQ^TQr_j=r_j^Tr_j=\|r_j\|^2.
$$
Taking nonnegative square roots proves $\boxed{\|a_j\|=\|r_j\|}$. This is simply norm preservation by an orthogonal <matrix>.

= d
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use a QR factorization, whose existence is supplied. Since $|\det Q|=1$ and $R$ is triangular,
$$
|\det A|=|\det R|=\prod_{j=1}^n|r_{jj}|.
$$
Each diagonal entry is one component of its column, so $|r_{jj}|\leq\|r_j\|=\|a_j\|$. Multiplication yields <Hadamard determinant inequality>:
$$
\boxed{|\det A|\leq\prod_{j=1}^n\|a_j\|.}
$$
The argument includes singular matrices, since a zero diagonal factor makes the <determinant> zero.

= e
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Every column has $n$ entries of magnitude at most one, so $\|a_j\|^2\leq n$. Substituting in part (d) gives
$$
\boxed{|\det A|\leq\prod_{j=1}^n\sqrt n=n^{n/2}.}
$$
The bound comes from the maximum allowed column lengths, together with Hadamard's inequality.
